+
+# Queue the welcome email after registration
+
+## Context
+
+Every newly registered account should receive a welcome message. Sending it
+from inside the registration request would tie sign-up to the mail provider:
+a slow provider makes registration hang, an unavailable one makes it fail —
+after the account has already been created.
+
+Registration was also the first feature in this app to need either a queue or
+a mailer, so neither subsystem was wired up.
+
+## Decision
+
+`RegisterController.store()` dispatches
+[SendWelcomeEmailJob](../../app/Jobs/SendWelcomeEmailJob.ts) once the account
+exists and the session is established, then redirects as before. The job
+composes [WelcomeMail](../../app/Mail/WelcomeMail.ts) and sends it wherever
+the queued work runs; a refused registration never reaches the dispatch.
+
+Both subsystems are selected by environment, so the same code serves
+development and production:
+
+- `QUEUE_CONNECTION` (see [config/queue.ts](../../config/queue.ts)) — `sync`
+  runs the job inline after dispatch, `memory` holds it for a worker, `redis`
+  is the production connection.
+- `MAIL_MAILER` (see [config/mail.ts](../../config/mail.ts)) — `log` writes
+  the message to the server output, `memory` records it for tests, `resend`
+  delivers it. The sender comes from `MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS` / `MAIL_FROM_NAME`.
+
+`QueueProvider` and `MailProvider` bind the two managers and install them
+where queued work reads them; `QueueProvider` also registers every job class,
+which is how a wire name resolves back to a class in a worker process.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Sign-up costs one queue write and never waits on the mail provider. A
+  delivery failure is retried by the queue (three attempts) and ends up as a
+  failed job instead of a failed registration — with `QUEUE_CONNECTION=sync`
+  there is no worker to retry it, so a development failure surfaces inline.
+- The payload carries the name and address rather than a user id, so the
+  worker composes the message without reading the database.
+- Any further job class has to be added to `QueueProvider`'s registration, or
+  the queued message cannot be resolved back to its class.
